If k is less than 4 to the power n and the X half-step atom at n and k is not junk, then the basic neighbourhood of its second-system index equals the intersection of the basic neighbourhood of the atom-pair's second index with the basic neighbourhood of the X-sequence code of n.

A point of the half-step neighbourhood lies in the atom-pair neighbourhood by the subset lemma, and the unconditional membership criterion for the X-sequence code, read backwards at the witness k, puts it in the sequence neighbourhood. Conversely, a point of both neighbourhoods lies in some non-junk half-step atom at a bit-source k' below 4 to the power n. If k' equals k, that membership is the claim. If not, both atom-pair junk flags vanish because a vanishing selection forces a vanishing flag, so some digit distinguishes the two bit-sources and the atom-pair codes are disjoint on the second side. The point lies in both second-index neighbourhoods, the one at k' by the subset lemma, contradicting that empty intersection.
